/*!
\page gislib_random_streams Caller-Owned Random Number Streams

\tableofcontents

\section gislib_random_streams_purpose Purpose

The G_random_*() functions give parallel code random numbers which do
not depend on the number of threads or on the order in which the threads
run. The shared generator behind G_drand48() hands each value to
whichever thread asks first, so a parallel run does not reproduce a
serial one, nor another parallel run. With G_random_seed_stream(), each
unit of work draws from a stream of its own, determined only by the
seed, the unit's number and the number of units, and a run gives the
same result with one thread as with many.

\section gislib_random_streams_ring The ring, streams, parts and stride

All 2^48 states of the generator lie on one cycle: a ring along which
each draw moves the generator by one state, and which closes after 2^48
draws. A stream is a stretch of that ring. G_random_seed_stream(state,
seed, stream, streams) cuts the ring into \c streams equal parts, or one
part more when that number is even, and places the state object at the
start of part number \c stream. The length of a part, which is also the
stride from the start of one stream to the start of the next, is what
the function returns.

\image html random_streams.svg "Four streams cut the ring into five parts, since four is even"

Two state objects seeded with the same seed and the same number of
streams but different stream numbers sit on different parts and never
produce the same values, as long as each draws fewer values than the
returned length. This is how independent objects, which share nothing
while they run, avoid each other: they are connected only through the
layout, that is the seed and the number of streams, and separated by
the stream number. Stream 0 starts where G_srand48() starts, so a single
stream reproduces the shared generator.

The extra part for an even number of streams, and the odd stride, keep
every pair of streams away from the power-of-two fractions of the ring,
where this generator repeats its values up to a constant. The
documentation of G_random_seed_stream() has the details.

\section gislib_random_streams_functions The three functions

G_random_seed_stream() seeds a state object to one stream of a layout,
G_random_seed() does the same for a layout with a single stream, and
G_random_double() draws the next value in [0, 1) from a state object:

\code
#include <grass/gis.h>

struct G_random_state state;
long long length;
double value;

length = G_random_seed_stream(&state, seed, stream, streams);
value = G_random_double(&state);
\endcode

A struct G_random_state is one generator object, 8 bytes holding a
position on the ring. The library does not tie it to a thread; the
caller decides what it belongs to. A state must be used by one thread at
a time. Seeding happens once per unit of work, when that unit's drawing
begins, and the thread number never appears in the arguments; that is
why the results do not depend on the schedule.

\section gislib_random_streams_unit Choosing the unit of work

The unit of work is what the computation is naturally divided into and
numbered by: a raster row, a point, an object, a group of particles, a
run of a model. The stream number is the unit's number and the number
of streams is the number of units. Which of the two patterns below
applies depends on whether a unit draws all of its values in one go or
in many pieces over time.

\section gislib_random_streams_once Pattern 1: a unit drawn in one go

When a unit of work is drawn in one go, for example one row of a raster,
one point or one object, each thread owns one state object. When the
thread takes a unit, it seeds its object to that unit's stream, draws
the unit's values, and when it takes the next unit seeds the same object
again to that unit's stream. The object's history does not matter: the
values depend only on the seed, the unit's number and the number of
units.

\code
#pragma omp parallel
{
    struct G_random_state state; /* One state per thread. */

#pragma omp for schedule(dynamic)
    for (long long unit = 0; unit < units; unit++) {
        G_random_seed_stream(&state, seed, unit, units);
        for (long long i = 0; i < values_per_unit; i++)
            result[unit][i] = G_random_double(&state);
    }
}
\endcode

Seeding costs about one hundred nanoseconds, roughly fifty draws, so
this is cheap unless a unit draws only a handful of values; then choose
a larger unit, for example a row rather than a cell.

\section gislib_random_streams_steps Pattern 2: a unit drawn in pieces over time

When a unit of work draws in many pieces over time, for example a group
of particles advanced through many time steps, or a component of a model
which draws at every step, the unit owns one state object for the whole
computation. The object is seeded once at the start to the unit's
stream, and drawn from at every step by whichever thread handles the
unit in that step; a thread may copy it into a private variable for the
step and copy it back. It is never seeded again, because the stream
must continue from step to step; seeding it again would return it to
the start of its part and repeat the same values in every step.

\code
struct G_random_state *states = G_malloc(units * sizeof(*states));

for (long long unit = 0; unit < units; unit++)
    G_random_seed_stream(&states[unit], seed, unit, units);

for (int step = 0; step < steps; step++) {
#pragma omp parallel for schedule(dynamic)
    for (long long unit = 0; unit < units; unit++)
        advance(unit, &states[unit]); /* Draws with G_random_double(). */
}
\endcode

\section gislib_random_streams_ensembles Ensembles

Several runs of the same computation under one seed, for example the
members of an ensemble, are laid out flat. With `runs` runs of `units`
units each, the stream of unit `unit` in run `run` is
`run * units + unit` out of `runs * units` streams, so the runs and
their units are all disjoint. Do not derive the runs from consecutive
seeds (seed + 1, seed + 2 and so on): for this generator, the values of
such runs step by a constant from one run to the next at every draw.

\section gislib_random_streams_limits Limits and the returned length

The streams together cannot exceed the period: the number of streams
times the number of values each draws must stay below 2^48, about
2.8e14. The returned length is the check; a stream drawn past its
length continues into the next stream and repeats that stream's values.
For a feel of the room:

- a million units drawing a million values each fit: each stream is
  281,474,695 values long, about 280 times what it draws;
- ten million units drawing ten thousand values each fit: 28,147,493
  values per stream, about 2,800 times;
- a billion units drawing ten thousand values each fit: 281,473 values
  per stream, about 28 times;
- a hundred billion units do not fit ten thousand values each: a stream
  is only 2,813 values long.

So one stream per row of a large raster, and one stream per particle of
a simulation with millions of particles, both fit. One stream per
particle for every run of a thousand-run ensemble with ten million
particles, ten billion streams of 28,147 values each, is at the edge;
that is where a generator with a longer period becomes necessary.

Compare the returned length with the number of values a unit will draw,
for example the columns times the draws per cell for a row, or the time
steps times the draws per step for a particle, and refuse the layout
with G_fatal_error() or warn with G_warning() when it does not fit.

\section gislib_random_streams_seeds Seeds

A seed lies between -2^31 and 2^32 - 1. The generator uses its low 32
bits, so a negative seed gives the same values as the seed 2^32 higher.
Seeds 2^31 apart give values shifted by exactly one half at every draw,
and seeds 2^30 apart by one quarter, so seeds which differ only in
their top bits are not independent; keep seeds below 2^30. Derive
several independent sequences from one seed with streams, never from
several seeds.

\section gislib_random_streams_migrating Migrating from the shared generator

Code which seeds with G_srand48(seed) and draws with G_drand48() gets
exactly the same values from a state seeded with G_random_seed(&state,
seed), which is stream 0 of a single stream, and drawn from with
G_random_double(&state). Existing results are therefore reproduced by a
single stream, and only by it: a layout with several streams changes
the values, since only stream 0 continues the shared sequence. Parallel
code whose values came from the shared generator changes them in any
case, because they depended on the schedule.

There are no caller-owned counterparts of G_lrand48() and G_mrand48().
The value of G_lrand48() is `(long)(G_random_double(&state) *
2147483648.0)`, exactly, since the state has 48 bits and a double holds
the product without rounding.

*/
